md('# Income–Consumption Gap and U.S. Household Demand\n\n**Historical 1959–2009 consumer-facing macro factor falsification** | Prepared 2026-10-28 | **NOT PIT / NOT INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC / NOT EQUITY ALPHA**\n\nThis is an independently readable, 15-chapter empirical paper. A real-consumption aggregate is *not* a direct measure of NAICS 44–45 retail sales, company fundamentals, or investable returns.')
md('## 01. Abstract\n\n**Primary question:** Does a one-quarter-stale real disposable-income growth premium over real household consumption growth improve four-quarter-ahead real personal consumption expenditure (PCE) growth forecasts beyond transparent baselines? The null is zero incremental forecast accuracy. All observations are a single 2009 snapshot, not historical point-in-time release vintages. We explicitly allow a negative result.')
code("from pathlib import Path\nimport json, numpy as np, pandas as pd\nfrom IPython.display import display, Image\nimport analysis as A\nROOT=Path.cwd(); raw=A.load_raw(); panel=A.build_panel(raw); pred=A.walk_forward(panel)\nmetrics=json.loads((ROOT/'results/metrics.json').read_text()); print('Snapshot rows:',len(raw),'Panel rows:',len(panel),'OOS origins:',len(pred))")
code("print('Historical period:',raw.date.iloc[0],raw.date.iloc[-1]); print('OOS period:',pred.date.iloc[0],pred.date.iloc[-1]);print('Evidence status:',metrics['status'])")
md('## 02. Research Design & Hypotheses\n\n**H1 (income catch-up):** household spending may adjust upward after income growth outpaces consumption growth. **H2 (incremental):** the gap improves forecast RMSE versus macro controls and a simple mean. **H3 (robustness):** improvement survives time dependence, vintage-buffer variation, economic regimes and factor-weight perturbations. Counterthesis: saving motives, credit conditions and shocks make the gap uninformative. Primary horizon is four quarters, with predeclared one-quarter observation buffer.')
code("print('Null: control-minus-augmented OOS RMSE <= 0; positive gain supports H2.');print('Horizon',A.BASE_HORIZON,'quarters; minimum train',A.MIN_TRAIN)")
code("print('Training target release purge: max(train origin) + horizon <= forecast origin:',bool((pred.train_last_origin+4<=pred.origin).all()))\ndisplay(pred[['date','origin','train_last_origin','train_n']].head(6))")
md('## 03. Industry Context\n\nThe transmission hypothesis is relevant to consumer-facing retail (NAICS 44–45), food service (722), recreation (71) and personal services (81), but the dependent variable is **aggregate real PCE across goods and services**. It is not an industry revenue series. The 2022 NAICS classification is used for mechanism mapping only and must not be treated as a 1959–2009 historical industry panel. Sector growth and firm exposure shares are UNKNOWN in the available sample.')
code("exposure=pd.read_csv('data/sector_exposure_map.csv');display(exposure[['sector','naics_2022','mechanism','quantitative_weight','empirically_validated']])")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/01_income_consumption_levels.png'))")
md('## 04. Data & Source Integrity\n\nThe frozen 203-row `statsmodels` U.S. macrodata snapshot has 1959Q1–2009Q3 observations, compiled from FRED and BLS with stated **access date 2009-12-15**. The access date is not an original publication date for each row. Levels are chained-2005-dollar billions at seasonally adjusted annual rates. Grade B for the bundled snapshot, grade A for official source/classification context, grade C for unverified qualitative sector exposure. The exact original quarterly FRED IDs and initial release timestamps are UNKNOWN.')
code("register=pd.read_csv('data/source_register.csv');display(register[['source_id','grade','publisher','collection_or_access_date','observation_period','revision_or_vintage_policy']])")
code("print('Raw missing cells:',int(raw.isna().sum().sum()),'Duplicate quarters:',int(raw.date.duplicated().sum()));display(raw[['date','realcons','realdpi','realgdp','unemp']].head())")
md('## 05. Point-in-Time Availability\n\nAt formation `t`, all signal and control observations are from `t−1` or earlier, and targets are from `t` through `t+4`. Train labels are restricted to `j+4 <= t`. These rules prevent *mechanical observation-date leakage*, but the **2009 revised snapshot cannot verify historical information sets**. BEA release lags vary and early-vintage observations are not recovered. This is explicitly NOT PIT.')
code("idx=20; t=int(panel.origin.iloc[idx]); print('Example origin',raw.date.iloc[t],'latest observed feature quarter',raw.date.iloc[t-1],'target endpoint',raw.date.iloc[t+4]);print('Feature',round(panel.factor.iloc[idx],4),'target',round(panel.target.iloc[idx],4))")
code("assert (pred.train_last_origin+4<=pred.origin).all();print('Purged OOS origins:',len(pred),'training sizes:',pred.train_n.min(),pred.train_n.max())")
md('## 06. Factor Construction\n\nDefine `g_DPI(t)=100[ln(DPI(t))-ln(DPI(t−4))]` and `g_PCE(t)` analogously. The fixed factor is **F(t)=g_DPI(t−1)−g_PCE(t−1)** in log-percentage points. No whole-sample z-score or fitted exposure weights are used. The dependent variable is `Y(t,4)=100[ln(PCE(t+4))-ln(PCE(t))]`. Weight perturbations vary the two factor coefficients, and never use future data in fitting.')
code("print('Factor summary:');display(panel[['factor','dpi_growth','cons_growth','target']].describe().round(3))")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/02_income_gap_factor.png'))")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/03_factor_outcome_scatter.png'))")
md('## 07. Exposure & Transmission\n\nA rise in real disposable income relative to real PCE could raise future consumption through a delayed spending response; alternatively, households may save more or service debt. Sector mappings are **qualitative**, with UNKNOWN historical revenue shares. There is no company universe, no historical company filing exposure and no security cross-section. Consequently, leave-one-company-out, fundamental IC and long–short return spreads are **not applicable**, rather than silently treated as zero.')
code("print('Sector mappings:',len(exposure),'numeric exposure weights observed:',exposure.quantitative_weight.ne('UNKNOWN').sum());display(exposure[['sector','reference','exposure_as_of']])")
md('## 08. Outcomes & Baselines\n\nAll models share the same purged expanding-window origins. Baselines: historical mean, one-quarter-stale PCE persistence scaled to horizon, consumption+unemployment AR Ridge, and multivariate Ridge controls (consumption growth, GDP growth, unemployment change, private investment growth). Challenger: controls plus the predeclared income–consumption gap. Ridge uses **training-only standardization** and fixed `alpha=2.0`; no hyperparameter search.')
code("score=A.scorecard(pred);display(score.round(4))")
code("display(pred[['date','target','historical_mean','consumption_persistence','macro_controls','macro_plus_factor']].head(8).round(3))")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/04_oos_rmse.png'))")
md('## 09. Empirical Findings\n\nPrimary effect is the RMSE difference `RMSE(macro controls) − RMSE(augmented)` in log-percentage points; positive values indicate improvement. Forecast errors are serially dependent because four-quarter targets overlap. Time-series factor–target correlation is descriptive and **not** a cross-sectional information coefficient. No equity return data are available.')
code("gain=A.rmse(pred.target,pred.macro_controls)-A.rmse(pred.target,pred.macro_plus_factor);print('Primary OOS RMSE improvement (log-pp):',round(gain,6));print('In-sample time-series factor/target Pearson correlation:',round(panel[['factor','target']].corr().iloc[0,1],4))")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/05_oos_forecasts.png'))")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/06_cumulative_loss.png'))")
md('## 10. Robustness & Placebos\n\nThe exploratory null shuffles intact eight-quarter blocks of the factor series, refits the expanding model 1,000 times, and reports the one-sided fraction of placebo gains at least as large as observed. Because blocks and the conditional null are approximate, this is **not** an exact randomization p-value. A 2,000-draw paired moving-block bootstrap resamples OOS forecast-error vectors in eight-quarter blocks. We also vary factor weights 1,000 times, perform ablations, vary horizons/lags and omit evaluation decades.')
code("pl=pd.read_csv('results/block_placebo_1000.csv');print('Block placebo draws:',len(pl),'Exploratory upper-tail fraction:',metrics['placebo_tail_fraction_exploratory']);display(pl.null_gain_log_pp.describe().round(4))")
code("bs=pd.read_csv('results/paired_block_bootstrap_2000.csv');print('Paired bootstrap draws:',len(bs),'95% CI:',metrics['bootstrap_ci95']);display(bs.gain_log_pp.describe().round(4))")
code("ex=pd.read_csv('results/exposure_perturbation_1000.csv');print('Factor perturbations:',len(ex),'positive-gain fraction:',metrics['exposure_gain_positive_fraction']);display(ex.gain_log_pp.describe().round(4))")
code("display(Image(filename='figures/07_block_placebo.png'));display(Image(filename='figures/08_bootstrap.png'))")
